WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.350
<v Instructor>Question five,</v>

2
00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:04.020
a company is launching a new web service

3
00:00:04.020 --> 00:00:06.480
on an Amazon ECS cluster.

4
00:00:06.480 --> 00:00:09.330
The company policy requires that the security group

5
00:00:09.330 --> 00:00:13.590
on the cluster instances block all inbound traffic

6
00:00:13.590 --> 00:00:17.400
but the port HTTPS, which is the port 443.

7
00:00:17.400 --> 00:00:21.900
The cluster consists of Amazon, 100 Amazon EC2 instances

8
00:00:21.900 --> 00:00:24.360
and security engineers are responsible

9
00:00:24.360 --> 00:00:27.120
for managing and updating the cluster instances.

10
00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.950
The security engineering team is small

11
00:00:28.950 --> 00:00:31.770
so any management efforts must be minimized.

12
00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.380
So here we have a requirement as a company policy

13
00:00:34.380 --> 00:00:36.990
that the security group cannot allow anything

14
00:00:36.990 --> 00:00:39.120
but inbound HTTPS.

15
00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:42.570
And then we have 100 EC2 instances in an ECS cluster.

16
00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:45.390
So let's see how we can go ahead to manage and update

17
00:00:45.390 --> 00:00:47.070
the cluster instances.

18
00:00:47.070 --> 00:00:49.560
So the architecture is, our ECS cluster

19
00:00:49.560 --> 00:00:51.690
has a bunch of EC2 instances in it

20
00:00:51.690 --> 00:00:55.050
and the client is able to access our EC2 instances

21
00:00:55.050 --> 00:00:57.330
only through going through a security group

22
00:00:57.330 --> 00:01:00.420
that is allowing inbound access on 443.

23
00:01:00.420 --> 00:01:04.200
So presumably this is how we access our HTTPS application

24
00:01:04.200 --> 00:01:05.910
onto our ECS cluster.

25
00:01:05.910 --> 00:01:08.850
And now we want to be able to go ahead in these instances

26
00:01:08.850 --> 00:01:10.230
and manage them.

27
00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:13.980
But again, the company policy is to have

28
00:01:13.980 --> 00:01:17.370
only the port 443 incoming into these instances.

29
00:01:17.370 --> 00:01:19.230
So if you follow closely to the course,

30
00:01:19.230 --> 00:01:20.730
originally the answer to the question,

31
00:01:20.730 --> 00:01:23.010
but let's look at the different options.

32
00:01:23.010 --> 00:01:26.370
Change the SSH port to 2222

33
00:01:26.370 --> 00:01:30.150
on the cluster instances with a user data script.

34
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:34.650
Log into each instance using SSH over port 2222.

35
00:01:34.650 --> 00:01:37.500
So it is definitely possible to,

36
00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.460
using a user data script to change the SSH port

37
00:01:41.460 --> 00:01:43.260
to another port 2222.

38
00:01:43.260 --> 00:01:45.840
And that is a common thing to do if you're

39
00:01:45.840 --> 00:01:47.070
want to enhance security

40
00:01:47.070 --> 00:01:50.001
to have a non-standard port for ssh.

41
00:01:50.001 --> 00:01:53.430
But the thing here is, here we cannot SSH

42
00:01:53.430 --> 00:01:56.580
into our instances because we would need to allow

43
00:01:56.580 --> 00:01:59.640
port 2222 into our security group,

44
00:01:59.640 --> 00:02:01.950
but that is against the company policy.

45
00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:05.520
So this would be a feasible solution, 100% for sure

46
00:02:05.520 --> 00:02:08.610
but the company policy would disable us

47
00:02:08.610 --> 00:02:12.900
from using the port 2222 to get into our EC2 instances.

48
00:02:12.900 --> 00:02:15.150
And so therefore, this option A

49
00:02:15.150 --> 00:02:17.910
looked like a viable solution, but it is not a good one

50
00:02:17.910 --> 00:02:19.590
because it violates the company policy.

51
00:02:19.590 --> 00:02:21.870
So we have to roll that out.

52
00:02:21.870 --> 00:02:25.020
Option B is to change yet again the SSH port

53
00:02:25.020 --> 00:02:28.560
using a user data script and then we use trusted advisor

54
00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:32.850
to remotely manage the cluster instances of our port 2222.

55
00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:36.840
So now we have trusted advisor that will be using that part.

56
00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:38.670
So two problems with this question.

57
00:02:38.670 --> 00:02:41.340
Number one, again, we have an incoming port

58
00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.250
into our security group that is against the company policy.

59
00:02:44.250 --> 00:02:47.430
So we can rule that out again for our option B.

60
00:02:47.430 --> 00:02:51.030
But trusted advisor is, if we remember correctly what it is,

61
00:02:51.030 --> 00:02:56.030
a service to help us get recommendations about our accounts.

62
00:02:56.250 --> 00:02:59.010
But in no way it is a service that would allow us

63
00:02:59.010 --> 00:03:01.830
to manage our EC2 instances actively.

64
00:03:01.830 --> 00:03:03.780
So Trusted Advisor is definitely

65
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:05.190
not the right service for this.

66
00:03:05.190 --> 00:03:07.380
It would give us cost recommendations

67
00:03:07.380 --> 00:03:10.200
security recommendations, performance recommendations

68
00:03:10.200 --> 00:03:11.400
all these kind of things

69
00:03:11.400 --> 00:03:14.430
but it would not help us to manage our instances

70
00:03:14.430 --> 00:03:18.810
and use actively, ports to SSH into our instances.

71
00:03:18.810 --> 00:03:22.290
So we have to rule out that answer for two reasons.

72
00:03:22.290 --> 00:03:24.870
Option C, launch the cluster instances

73
00:03:24.870 --> 00:03:26.640
with no SSH key pairs.

74
00:03:26.640 --> 00:03:29.970
That is definitely an option in AWS.

75
00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:33.780
And then we use the Amazon EC2 systems manager run command

76
00:03:33.780 --> 00:03:36.660
to remotely manage the cluster instances.

77
00:03:36.660 --> 00:03:37.493
So let's have a look,

78
00:03:37.493 --> 00:03:39.540
I just made an instance a little bit bigger.

79
00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.460
So we have our ECS cluster security group

80
00:03:41.460 --> 00:03:44.850
and our EC2 instance accessible of the port 443.

81
00:03:44.850 --> 00:03:49.080
And we use the SSM agent inside of our EC2 instance

82
00:03:49.080 --> 00:03:53.880
and use the SSM run command to access our EC2 instances.

83
00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:57.300
So that is 100% a viable option.

84
00:03:57.300 --> 00:04:00.060
I wish this option was a bit more clear

85
00:04:00.060 --> 00:04:02.820
because to use the systems manager run command

86
00:04:02.820 --> 00:04:07.200
we need to make sure before that two things are there.

87
00:04:07.200 --> 00:04:10.110
Number one, we need to make sure the SSM agent is running

88
00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:11.730
onto our EC2 instance

89
00:04:11.730 --> 00:04:13.680
which is not the case for every EC2 instance.

90
00:04:13.680 --> 00:04:16.140
You need to install it most of the time.

91
00:04:16.140 --> 00:04:17.760
Or if you use Amazon Linux 2

92
00:04:17.760 --> 00:04:19.140
then it's already installed for you

93
00:04:19.140 --> 00:04:20.010
but you would need to install it

94
00:04:20.010 --> 00:04:21.990
if you are using other operating systems.

95
00:04:21.990 --> 00:04:23.850
So that's number one, the big problem.

96
00:04:23.850 --> 00:04:25.050
And number two is,

97
00:04:25.050 --> 00:04:28.170
for your EC2 instance running the SSM agent

98
00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:31.800
to be managed by SSM, the service itself,

99
00:04:31.800 --> 00:04:33.840
you would need to make sure your EC2 instance

100
00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:37.230
has an instance role that allows us to access the EC2,

101
00:04:37.230 --> 00:04:40.350
the EC2 systems manager service.

102
00:04:40.350 --> 00:04:43.470
So there are two things missing in that option.

103
00:04:43.470 --> 00:04:46.650
So this is the correct option for this question

104
00:04:46.650 --> 00:04:48.360
but I think it's really incomplete.

105
00:04:48.360 --> 00:04:50.760
Now that is something that does happen in the exam.

106
00:04:50.760 --> 00:04:52.560
Sometimes answer are incomplete,

107
00:04:52.560 --> 00:04:54.720
I would say most often not they're not incomplete,

108
00:04:54.720 --> 00:04:56.370
but sometimes that can happen.

109
00:04:56.370 --> 00:04:59.790
So here this is the right answer, but slightly incomplete.

110
00:04:59.790 --> 00:05:02.130
But here, using the run commands

111
00:05:02.130 --> 00:05:04.950
we can manage a fleet of EC2 instances

112
00:05:04.950 --> 00:05:08.610
and update them all at once without getting the need

113
00:05:08.610 --> 00:05:11.310
to open up any port on our instances.

114
00:05:11.310 --> 00:05:14.580
And the reason is the SSM agent will be pulling

115
00:05:14.580 --> 00:05:18.090
the systems manager service and not the other way.

116
00:05:18.090 --> 00:05:20.040
So this is the correct option.

117
00:05:20.040 --> 00:05:23.670
Now, option D, launch the cluster instances with no SSH key

118
00:05:23.670 --> 00:05:26.970
and use trusted advisor to manage the instance,

119
00:05:26.970 --> 00:05:27.960
that makes no sense.

120
00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.720
We cannot use trusted advisor to manage instances

121
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:32.460
so we rule out that answer.

122
00:05:32.460 --> 00:05:35.580
So remember the correct answer here is option C,

123
00:05:35.580 --> 00:05:39.300
with the little asterisk that we would need an IAM role

124
00:05:39.300 --> 00:05:41.790
and also the SSM agent properly configured

125
00:05:41.790 --> 00:05:43.650
and running for this to work.

126
00:05:43.650 --> 00:05:44.483
But that's it.

127
00:05:44.483 --> 00:05:47.380
I hope you liked it and I will see you in the next lecture.

